title: Sending files to the trash
tags: Trash
kind: guide
summary: Trash a tracked file or directory from the command line, a metarecord panel or the file manager.

!! From the command line

```
mf trash -f <path>
```

<<cmd "mf trash">> `-f` trashes a ''tracked'' file or directory — a safe delete. It
refuses, before touching anything, when the daemon is not running or when the
path has no metarecord: an untracked file is not the trash's business. A
directory goes whole, with every metarecord under it.

When another metarecord still points at one of those being trashed (a `ref` or
`tree_ref` field), the trashing is refused and the referrers are named; add
`--force` to trash anyway and leave those references dangling.

!! From a metarecord panel

In the `metarecord-list`, `metarecord-detail` and `treeref` panels,
on the selected metarecord:

* <<key "metarecord:remove" Delete>> — <<cmd "metarecord:remove">>: if the metarecord has a file, asks whether to trash it (OK) or keep it and delete the metarecord alone (Cancel); without a file, deletes the metarecord.
* <<key "metarecord:trash" Ctrl+Delete>> — <<cmd "metarecord:trash">>: trashes the file at once (confirmed).
* <<key "metarecord:delete" Shift+Delete>> — <<cmd "metarecord:delete">>: deletes the metarecord and keeps the file.

The metarecord panels also offer ''Move to trash'' in their context menu ([[Context menus]]).

!! Several at once

`metarecord:bulk <target> trash` trashes the files of a whole set
([[Editing metarecords in the GUI]]): the checked metarecords
(<<key "metarecord:bulk selection trash" "m m t">>) or everything the
[[metarecord-list panel]] shows (<<key "metarecord:bulk query trash" "m q t">>). It
asks first, naming the count, then:

* captures every metarecord of the set and deletes them all in ''one'' revision — one undo, and a reference from one member of the set to another is not a refusal ([[What trashing does]]);
* moves each file or directory into the trash as an entry of its own, restored one by one ([[Restoring from the trash]]);
* leaves out what has no file (a metarecord without `mfr_path`, an orphan), what lies under a directory of the set (it goes with the directory), and the repository root.

A refusal from the daemon happens before a single byte has moved. The status line
says how many files went and what was left out.

!! From the file manager

In the [[file-manager panel]], deleting the highlighted entry sends it to the
repository's trash when a repository is active (its metarecords go with it if it
is tracked, the bytes alone if it is not); with no active repository the removal
is permanent. Both are confirmed.

The GUI never forces a trashing past a reference: the refusal is shown, and
`mf trash -f --force` is the way through.

Then: [[Restoring from the trash]], or [[Trash retention]].
